{
  "_comment": "PodMotion agent CRIU-scoped seccomp profile (ADR-0039 SD-5, ADR-0148; ptrace model corrected Sprint 37 M22). Default-deny (SCMP_ACT_ERRNO, errno EPERM=1). Allows the CRIU checkpoint/restore syscall surface plus the Go runtime; denies privilege-escalation, kernel-image-load, keyring, NUMA-policy, perf, and io_uring syscalls outright (they fall through to the default ERRNO action). ptrace(2) is filtered ordered-first-match: the PEEK/POKE memory-injection request family (PTRACE_PEEKTEXT=1, PEEKDATA=2, PEEKUSR=3, POKETEXT=4, POKEDATA=5, POKEUSR=6) is DENIED (ERRNO EPERM) by explicit rules placed BEFORE a final blanket ptrace ALLOW. First-matching rule wins in OCI/runc seccomp (rules processed in syscalls-array order), so injection requests EPERM while every other ptrace request \u2014 including the control requests CRIU needs (PTRACE_SEIZE/INTERRUPT/ATTACH/DETACH/GET*REGSET) \u2014 passes to the kernel. PTRACE_ATTACH(16) MUST be allowed: CRIU's kerndat probe kerndat_has_ptrace_get_rseq_conf() -> fork_and_ptrace_attach() (criu/util.c:740) uses the legacy ptrace(PTRACE_ATTACH) at startup, not PTRACE_SEIZE; the prior per-value allowlist omitted 16 and EPERM'd that probe (kerndat.c:2098). ATTACH only stops the tracee and establishes the trace relationship \u2014 it does not read or write tracee memory; the injection surface is the PEEK/POKE family, which remains denied. CRIU reads/writes target memory via process_vm_readv/process_vm_writev (separately allowlisted), not via ptrace PEEK/POKE. The agent's ptrace reach is additionally bounded by capability scope and hostPID:false (ADR-0148 D5) \u2014 this arg-filter is defense-in-depth on top of that. The new-mount-API syscalls (move_mount, open_tree, fsopen, fsmount, fsconfig, fspick) are allowed for CRIU 4.x kerndat init. CAP_CHECKPOINT_RESTORE (cap 40) must also be present on the agent. fork (syscall 57) and vfork are allowlisted (ADR-0229): CRIU's cr_system() (criu/util.c:657) fork()s /bin/sh to run tar for tmpfs-content serialization (criu/filesystems.c:426 Can't dump tmpfs content otherwise); clone(56)/clone3(435) were present but fork is a distinct x86-64 syscall and was EPERM'd by the SCMP_ACT_ERRNO default, surfacing as '/bin/sh: Cannot fork' and 'tar: Child returned status 2'. wait4/waitpid are present so the parent reaps the tar child. Validate against an actual CRIU strace before production.",
